not executor residue. No failed services, unsafe exposure, or damaged package state observed in the after_executor snapshot. All three restarts were performed sequentially with clear pre/post verification, consistent with a bounded, purposeful operation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-35b729dc8b504c0e","cmd-50e4de24a0a44a38","cmd-32d763b34eb84e23","cmd-f2bde6df713943eb","cmd-cbdd72a8c0674635","cmd-85c51a1d41814bf4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence: node1 (OPNsense/unbound) resolves normal names correctly for node2 and node3, blocked.example and its subdomains return nothing usable (NXDOMAIN) from both client hosts, and node2/node3 were explicitly reconfigured to use node1 as their DNS server rather than merely being able to reach it. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also fully and coherently demonstrated for all three nodes — baseline, disruption, changed boot identity, and recovered DNS/service state — and is corroborated by the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: mutations were purposeful and bounded, with only one failed (non-mutating) command attempt and no lingering residue detected in the final lifecycle snapshot.","limitations":["Blocking behavior was verified with a handful of representative subdomains (www., anything., foo., sub.) rather than an exhaustive subdomain space; deeper multi-label subdomains were not explicitly tested.","The 'after_prepare' snapshot is an alias of 'before_prepare' (prepare was disabled), so preparer-vs-executor separation relies entirely on the executor's own before/after captures.","DNS resolution verification relied on ephemeral resolvectl/drill queries rather than a broader battery of domains, though this is standard and sufficient for the stated exception-based policy."]},"ttft